From 80651d8f4bad3ffca4cb422d1272e10b412d1cfb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 20:52:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] volume_id: an HFS+ block size must be a power of two of at
 least 512

volume_id_probe_hfs_hfsplus() reads the block size of an HFS+ volume
header and divides by it to find the catalog leaf block:

	leaf_block = (leaf_node_head * leaf_node_size) / blocksize;

A volume header with a block size of 0 makes this a division by zero
(SIGFPE), so a crafted partition stops whatever is probing the
devices: blkid and mount with LABEL=/UUID= in the initramfs of
LibreELEC, which scan every block device at boot, and findfs in the
system. This is CVE-2026-124743 (CNA Red Hat, 2026-11-11), for which
there is no BusyBox fix yet.

util-linux's libblkid has accepted only a power of two of at least
512 there for years (libblkid/src/superblocks/hfs.c in 2.42.4), and
returns "not this filesystem" otherwise. Do the same: such a header is
not a valid HFS+ volume.
---
 util-linux/volume_id/hfs.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/util-linux/volume_id/hfs.c b/util-linux/volume_id/hfs.c
index 05d16db..5a5fda4 100644
--- a/util-linux/volume_id/hfs.c
+++ b/util-linux/volume_id/hfs.c
@@ -238,6 +238,10 @@ int FAST_FUNC volume_id_probe_hfs_hfsplus(struct volume_id *id /*,uint64_t off*/
 
 	blocksize = be32_to_cpu(hfsplus->blocksize);
 	dbg("blocksize %u", blocksize);
+	/* It divides below, and a sector is 512 bytes: like libblkid,
+	 * accept only a power of two of at least 512 */
+	if (blocksize < 512 || (blocksize & (blocksize - 1)) != 0)
+		return -1;
 
 	memcpy(extents, hfsplus->cat_file.extents, sizeof(extents));
 	cat_block = be32_to_cpu(extents[0].start_block);
